= Belief — Simple =

A '''belief''' is when you take something to be true or think that something is the case.

You do not need to be thinking about a belief all the time. You can believe that the Earth orbits the Sun while you are sleeping, eating, or thinking about something else.

Belief is not the same as [[Knowledge/Simple|knowledge]]. You can believe something that is wrong. You can also believe something true only because you guessed correctly.

Beliefs can have different strengths. You might be very confident that it will rain, slightly confident, or unsure. Philosophers often call a degree of confidence a '''credence'''.

Belief is also different from '''acceptance'''. Sometimes a person uses an assumption for a task without fully believing it. Scientists do this when they use simplified models.

Beliefs can come from perception, memory, reasoning, other people, or experience. [[Epistemology/Simple|Epistemology]] studies when beliefs are reasonable, justified, or count as knowledge.

